    "/api/v1/data-snapshot/orphans": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Defensive integrity audit of \`address_snapshot.customer_id\` → \`customer_snapshot\` and \`container_snapshot.pickup_address_id\` → \`address_snapshot\`. D1 enforces both foreign keys on writes and parent deletes, so normal streamed syncs cannot create orphans. A nonzero report warrants investigating legacy or out-of-band data and missing or mismatched schema constraints. Returns per-edge orphan counts plus deterministic id samples (ordered by primary key) from one read-only transaction. Same bearer as other snapshot routes.",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "description": "Max ids sampled per edge (counts are unaffected). Out-of-range values are rejected before D1 is touched.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "sampleLimit",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "default": 100,
              "description": "Max ids sampled per edge (counts are unaffected). Out-of-range values are rejected before D1 is touched.",
              "example": 100,
              "maximum": 500,
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer",
            },
          },
        ],
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "example": {
                  "orphanAddresses": {
                    "count": 0,
                    "sampleIds": [],
                  },
                  "orphanContainers": {
                    "count": 0,
                    "sampleIds": [],
                  },
                },
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/SnapshotOrphanReport",
                },
              },
            },
            "description": "Orphan counts and id samples per FK edge",
          },
          "400": {
            "content": {
              "application/problem+json": {
                "schema": {
                  "properties": {
                    "detail": {
                      "type": "string",
                    },
                    "status": {
                      "enum": [
                        400,
                      ],
                      "type": "number",
                    },
                    "title": {
                      "type": "string",
                    },
                    "type": {
                      "type": "string",
                    },
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "type",
                    "title",
                    "status",
                    "detail",
                  ],
                  "type": "object",
                },
              },
            },
            "description": "Invalid sampleLimit (application/problem+json). Must be an integer from 1 to 500; invalid values are rejected before any D1 query runs.",
          },
          "401": {
            "description": "Unauthorized",
          },
        },
        "summary": "Report orphan rows on the snapshot FK chain (D1)",
        "tags": [
          "data-snapshot",
        ],
      },
    },
    "/api/v1/data-snapshot/sync": {
      "post": {
        "description": "Runs **customer** → **address** → **container** when the corresponding \`*_REPORT_EXPORT_PATH\` or \`*_REPORT_QUERY_ID\` vars are set. Requires \`Authorization: Bearer <CUSTOMER_SNAPSHOT_SYNC_SECRET>\`. This route **chains three internal HTTP calls** to the per-table \`...-snapshot/sync\` routes (via the request origin) so each table runs in a **separate** Worker invocation (D1’s per-invocation operation limit applies per table, not to the whole job). The daily cron uses the same chained path only when \`SHADOW_API_PUBLIC_BASE\` is set (otherwise it falls back to one single-invocation run — see \`src/scheduled.ts\`). Each table is streamed; see \`CUSTOMER_SNAPSHOT.md\`. Pass \`?dryRun=true\` to dry-run every table (the flag is forwarded to each chained subrequest): full parse/validate, would-be counts, no D1 or R2 writes. Pass \`?partialProgress=true\` to opt into sanitized partial progress on a table failure: HTTP 500 \`application/json\` \`{customer, address, container, failure}\` with per-table \`{upserted, rowCount}\` (completed) or \`null\` and fixed \`{table, code}\` plus numeric \`status\` only for \`ChainedHttpError\` (\`code\` is one of the six runbook §4.1 kinds, never raw upstream text). Default and \`?partialProgress=false\` keep the generic problem+json shapes byte-for-byte; success is identical with or without the flag.

**Try it in \`/docs\`:** Authorize with the sync secret once, then Send with an empty body — this is the highest-friction flow made one click. Per-table 200s carry \`{upserted, rowCount}\` only (no \`deleted\` leak); a table with no export source comes back \`null\`, not zeros.",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "description": "Dry run: run validation only and persist nothing — no upstream write, no D1 write, no R2 write, no cache invalidation. Mutating snapshot POSTs reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "dryRun",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "description": "Dry run: run validation only and persist nothing — no upstream write, no D1 write, no R2 write, no cache invalidation. Mutating snapshot POSTs reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
              "enum": [
                "true",
                "false",
              ],
              "example": "true",
              "type": "string",
            },
          },
          {
            "description": "Partial progress: on a table failure answer 500 with per-table {upserted,rowCount}|null counts plus a fixed {table,code[,status]} failure instead of the generic problem+json. Any other query key is rejected with a value-free 400 before any table runs.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "partialProgress",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "description": "Partial progress: on a table failure answer 500 with per-table {upserted,rowCount}|null counts plus a fixed {table,code[,status]} failure instead of the generic problem+json. Any other query key is rejected with a value-free 400 before any table runs.",
              "enum": [
                "true",
                "false",
              ],
              "example": "true",
              "type": "string",
            },
          },
        ],
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "example": {
                  "address": {
                    "rowCount": 2,
                    "upserted": 2,
                  },
                  "container": {
                    "rowCount": 2,
                    "upserted": 2,
                  },
                  "customer": {
                    "rowCount": 2,
                    "upserted": 2,
                  },
                },
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/DataSnapshotSyncResult",
                },
              },
            },
            "description": "Per-table Result (null if that export path is empty or was skipped for FKs)",
          },
          "401": {
            "description": "Unauthorized",
          },
        },
        "summary": "Fetch all configured Routeware CSV reports and upsert D1 (ordered)",
        "tags": [
          "data-snapshot",
        ],
      },
    },
    "/api/v1/data/address": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Requires \`Authorization: Bearer <CUSTOMER_SNAPSHOT_SYNC_SECRET>\`. Read aggregate from the daily snapshot DB. Caching: \`ETag\` / \`If-None-Match\` and the Workers **Cache** API; invalidated after successful POST. Query: \`id\` = \`pickup_address_id\` (alias \`pickupAddressId\`). Batch: \`ids=1,2,3\` hydrates up to 50 records as \`{entries}\` in request order (\`no-store\`, no ETag); \`ids\` cannot be combined with \`id\`/alias. Optional \`fields=city,address\` projects only the \`.address\` root plus \`pickup_address_id\` on single-ID reads; related data remains unchanged unless a typed selector below names its type. Optional \`fields[customer]\`, \`fields[address]\`, \`fields[container]\` project every object of that type — the root and all nested relations — from the same per-type allowlists; omitted types stay full, every projected object keeps its primary id, and \`fields\` plus \`fields[address]\` together are a 400. Omitted selectors preserve the full response. ETags hash the served bytes for each representation; projected validators never reuse a full-only validator, and the canonical full cache is never overwritten by a projection. Field selectors apply to single-ID reads; combining \`ids\` with \`fields\` or any \`fields[type]\` is a value-free 400. Unknown query keys are a value-free 400 before any cache or D1 work.",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "description": "Canonical positive decimal safe integer, from 1 to 9007199254740991, without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every supplied spelling must be valid; id wins over the alias. Hydrated entity reads reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "id",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "description": "Canonical positive decimal safe integer, from 1 to 9007199254740991, without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every supplied spelling must be valid; id wins over the alias. Hydrated entity reads reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
              "example": 10,
              "maximum": 9007199254740991,
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer",
            },
          },
          {
            "description": "Canonical positive decimal safe integer, from 1 to 9007199254740991, without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every supplied spelling must be valid; id wins over the alias. Hydrated entity reads reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "pickupAddressId",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "description": "Canonical positive decimal safe integer, from 1 to 9007199254740991, without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every supplied spelling must be valid; id wins over the alias. Hydrated entity reads reject any other query key with a value-free 400 before any work.",
              "example": 10,
              "maximum": 9007199254740991,
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer",
            },
          },
          {
            "description": "Batch lookup as one comma-separated \`ids\` value: 1 to 50 canonical positive decimal safe integers (1 to 9007199254740991) without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every entry is validated before any read; any malformed or empty entry, mixing with \`id\`/alias/\`fields\`/\`fields[type]\`, or more than 50 ids fails the whole request 400 with zero partial reads. Batch 200s return \`{entries}\` in request order with \`no-store\` and no ETag.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "ids",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "description": "Batch lookup as one comma-separated \`ids\` value: 1 to 50 canonical positive decimal safe integers (1 to 9007199254740991) without leading zeros, whitespace, signs, fractions, exponents or hex. Every entry is validated before any read; any malformed or empty entry, mixing with \`id\`/alias/\`fields\`/\`fields[type]\`, or more than 50 ids fails the whole request 400 with zero partial reads. Batch 200s return \`{entries}\` in request order with \`no-store\` and no ETag.",
              "example": "1,2,3",
              "type": "string",
            },
          },
          {
            "description": "Comma-separated sparse fieldset: exact stored column names from this route's allowlist (case-sensitive, no spaces, no empty segments, no duplicates, single key only). Response rows contain exactly these fields plus the resource id, which is always present even when omitted. Unknown names are a value-free 400 before any D1 work.",
            "in": "query",
            "name": "fields",
